## [:< ##

# name  = editor.control.cycler.window_width
# descr = fixed display-column budget for a windowed cycler render
# return = a bare integer

## a SINGLE source both user-edit.form.build_frame's width RESERVATION and
## editor.ui.ascii_frame.render_form's render-time width BUDGET read directly,
## rather than either deriving one from the other -- see the 'user-edit form:
## horizontal-scroll add-a-field cycler' design for why deriving the
## render-time budget from min_width [ the multiline viewport fix's own
## pattern, editor.control.multiline.viewport_slice ] is circular for THIS row
## specifically.
##
## the multiline derivation works only because build_frame's own
## $display_length returns ZERO for a multiline field -- min_width there is
## genuinely computed from everything ELSE on the form. the add-a-field row is
## not a bystander the same way : build_frame pushes it into @current_rows
## even when the schema does not carry it yet, specifically because it is
## normally the WIDEST row in the form -- it is one of min_width's own
## drivers. shrinking its build-time contribution while still deriving its
## render-time width FROM that same min_width would be circular : a sparse
## form's min_width could drop below what even one choice cell needs, the
## render-time floor would silently win, and the row would render wider than
## the frame reserved -- exactly the jump-on-focus class this whole
## reservation system exists to prevent.
##
## a fixed constant, read by both sides, cannot disagree with itself.
##
## tuned empirically against live rendering, same as editor.control.list.
## window_cap and plugin.user-edit.address-cluster.render's TAB_WIDTH were --
## not a structural invariant, just a number that reads well.

return 40;

